{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"A 2020 US physician survey (published Jan 2025) finds nearly all NGS-ordering oncologists, surgeons and pathologists report barriers, led by reimbursement rather than sequencing cost.","detail":"Future Oncology 2025;21(2):231-239, n=200 US physicians surveyed Oct–Dec 2020. 99.5% reported barriers. Reimbursement challenges were cited by 87.5%, knowledge gaps about methods by 81.0%, and limited clinical-utility evidence by 80.0%. Cost per Gb is not among the top-ranked barriers in the summaries I found. I read this from a search-result summary because PubMed itself returned a cookie wall.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39606845/","date":"2025-01 (data Oct–Dec 2020)","source_name":"Future Oncology / PubMed 39606845","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Gives the actual percentages (87.5/81.0/80.0) behind the known qualitative 'reimbursement, not sequencing cost' point."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Prior authorization is the single most-cited reimbursement obstacle to NGS ordering, so demand is gated downstream of the sequencer.","detail":"In the same 200-physician survey, prior authorization for NGS was cited by 72.0%. Uncertainty about new fee codes was cited by 68.0% and paperwork/administrative duties by 67.5%. All three are non-sequencing constraints, consistent with the thesis that cheaper Gb does not unlock volume. The data are from 2020, so they are stale.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39606845/","date":"2025-01 (data 2020)","source_name":"Future Oncology / PubMed 39606845","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Quantifies prior-authorization burden (72%) for NGS ordering physicians; not in the known list."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Payer denials of lab, molecular and genomics claims rose sharply in 2025, a rising non-sequencing-cost brake on clinical volume.","detail":"XiFin's 2026 Payor Denial Impact Report (published Apr 2026) analyzed more than 30M claims. The blended denial rate for clinical lab, molecular and genomics claims was 11.2% in 2025 vs 8.0% in 2024, a 40% relative rise. Traditional Medicare was the lowest at 3.4%. The rate is blended across segments, not molecular-only. XiFin sells revenue-cycle services, so its sample reflects its own customer base.","url":"https://clpmag.com/?p=92893","date":"2026-04","source_name":"XiFin 2026 Payor Denial Impact Report via CLP Magazine","source_type":"claims_data","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Claims-based 2025 denial series (8.0%→11.2%) is not in the known file, which has only the CMS price-cut and HHS-OIG items."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Prior authorization's share of molecular and genetic testing denials more than doubled from 2021 to 2025.","detail":"In XiFin's data, prior-authorization denials were 13.1% of molecular/genetic denials in 2021 and 27.6% in 2025 (×2.1 in four years, about 20% per year compounded: (27.6/13.1)^(1/4)=1.205). The figure is a share of denials, not a denial rate. It shows payer friction is growing in exactly the high-Gb clinical categories Illumina's customers sell. XiFin's trade summary said payers had pledged to ease prior authorization, but this data does not show it.","url":"https://clpmag.com/?p=92893","date":"2026-04","source_name":"XiFin 2026 Payor Denial Impact Report via CLP Magazine","source_type":"claims_data","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Time-series quantification of prior-authorization friction for molecular testing; new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Bundling edits, which pay nothing separately for lab procedures, now account for most lab denials, a pricing and volume headwind for labs.","detail":"'Procedure not paid separately' bundling was 59% of clinical-lab denials in the 2026 report vs 33% in 2018. It exceeded 61% of denials at hospital outreach labs. This is a payer-side revenue squeeze on test volume that has nothing to do with sequencing cost. It also makes customers more price-sensitive on COGS.","url":"https://clpmag.com/?p=92893","date":"2026-04","source_name":"XiFin 2026 Payor Denial Impact Report via CLP Magazine","source_type":"claims_data","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Adds a payer-edit time series (33%→59%) for lab claims; not in the known file."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"A Yale single-center analysis finds 20% of solid-tumor NGS tests were denied, and most denials were administrative, so friction caps volume independent of Gb cost.","detail":"Korie et al., USCAP 2026 (Lab Invest supplement), via Bruce Quinn's AI-drafted blog summary of 27 Mar 2026. Of 1,392 solid-tumor NGS tests from 2022–23, 275 (20%) were denied. By cancer type, lung was 30%, head and neck 11% and colorectal 8%. 78% of denied cases carried incorrect ICD-10 codes. Of denials, 34% had no prior authorization and 25% were 'not medically necessary'. This is one center and a secondary summary; I could not open the original abstract.","url":"https://www.discoveriesinhealthpolicy.com/2026/03/korie-et-al-2026-what-drives-next-gen.html","date":"2026-03-27","source_name":"Discoveries in Health Policy (Quinn) / USCAP 2026","source_type":"conference","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Gives first claims-level NGS denial rate (20%) and its drivers; the known file has none."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"The Yale authors frame denials as fixable with coding and workflow changes, so the denial drag may be removable rather than structural.","detail":"The blog summary says 96% of ordering-clinician coding was correct and 100% of pathology-report coding was correct. 78% of denials traced to ICD-10 errors downstream in billing, and 80% of tests were physician-ordered rather than reflex. If labs fix coding, about 20% of tests are recoverable, which would support volume. The data are from 2022–23.","url":"https://www.discoveriesinhealthpolicy.com/2026/03/korie-et-al-2026-what-drives-next-gen.html","date":"2026-03-27","source_name":"Discoveries in Health Policy (Quinn) / USCAP 2026","source_type":"conference","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Watch-out: the denial friction is operational and fixable."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Successful appeals of molecular claims recover large dollars per case, which gives labs a path to retain volume and revenue despite denials.","detail":"XiFin 2025 data show an average recovery of $1,684 per successful molecular/genetic appeal vs $235 for pathology, $171 for clinical lab and $48 for hospital outreach. Appeals take two to three cycles at 20+ minutes of staff time each. High-value molecular claims therefore justify the effort, so denial friction is more likely to delay than to destroy volume.","url":"https://clpmag.com/?p=92893","date":"2026-04","source_name":"XiFin 2026 Payor Denial Impact Report via CLP Magazine","source_type":"claims_data","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Appeal-recovery economics for molecular claims; not in the known file."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Natera's CEO said 30–35% of Signatera volume is for indications not covered by Medicare's MolDX program, which limits how fast MRD volume can monetize.","detail":"On the Q4'25 call (Feb 2026), Natera said it has a 'full suite' of Signatera submissions under MolDX review. About 30–35% of Signatera volume is in indications not yet covered. FY25 oncology tests were ~800,800 vs ~528,200 in FY24 (+51.6%). Coverage, not sequencing cost, sets the ASP and the economics of that volume. This is from a search-result snippet; I did not open the call transcript.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1604821/000110465926020881/ntra-20251231x10k.htm","date":"2026-02","source_name":"Natera Q4'25 call via search summary; FY25 10-K","source_type":"earnings_call","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The 30–35% uncovered share is a number the known file lacks; the known file only has the ASP-uplift timing."},
